# Gateway certificate inventory and expiry reads

Certificate expiry is a read-only inventory check, not certificate issuance,
renewal, revocation checking or TLS-chain validation. It requires firewall.view
and preserves the existing organization, active-parent and site-access checks.
No certificate or gateway configuration is changed by these routes.

GET /firewall/gateways/{id}/certificates/certs returns a confirmed inventory.
GET /firewall/gateways/{id}/certificates/expiry classifies that inventory using
an expiry threshold of 1 through 365 days (the dashboard requests 30).

Unsupported adapter operations return 501. Failed connections, failed reads,
malformed envelopes and incomplete inventories return a generic 502. Neither
condition means there are zero certificates or that certificates are healthy.
Raw device errors, private keys and certificate payloads are not returned by the
OPNsense inventory adapter; retry diagnostics retain only the exception type.

The OPNsense implementation calls the Trust cert/search endpoint with all rows
requested. It validates the row list, unique record identities, current page,
returned count and total count before normalizing public metadata. Firmware
with a different or incomplete response fails visibly; compatibility must be
verified against each supported firmware in the operator's environment.

Expiry classification supports ISO timestamps, legacy UTC timestamps and
OPNsense Unix seconds, including numeric strings and zero. Missing, malformed,
non-finite or out-of-range dates are unknown, not valid. The response includes
unknown and unknown_count, and needs_attention is true for unknown, expired or
soon-expiring certificates. Every returned certificate is counted exactly once.

The dashboard verifies the gateway identity, threshold, row shapes and counts.
It hides cached status during refresh and after errors, offers an explicit retry,
and distinguishes an empty inventory from a healthy nonempty inventory. Unknown
expiry rows never show a fabricated date. English, Spanish and Chinese include
the unavailable, empty and unknown states.

The automated checks use real PostgreSQL, ASGI, the actual OPNsense client/parser,
and a real Chromium page with synthetic device responses. These do not prove
live firmware compatibility, certificate deployment, unattended renewal, trust
chain health, continuous freshness, HA or enterprise acceptance. The separate
trust overview, CA/CRL and ACME summaries have distinct contracts and acceptance
limits described in TRUST-ACME-INVENTORY-READS; an expiry pass does not qualify them.
